# The build context is the whole repo (a monorepo needs it). Everything listed here is NOT
# sent to Docker. That makes builds faster, keeps secrets out of images, and stops files from
# the host from overwriting files made inside the image.

# 1. Installed packages. The image runs its own `pnpm install`. Host copies are for the host's
#    operating system (macOS) and would break Linux-native packages like @node-rs/argon2.
**/node_modules

# 2. Build output. The image builds its own. Stale host output could be copied in by mistake.
**/.next
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/next-env.d.ts

# 3. Env files hold local settings and passwords. They must never end up in an image layer.
#    Settings are given to a container when it RUNS (docker-compose.yml), not when it is built.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# 4. Test output: big, changes on every test run, and not needed to run the app.
**/test-results
**/playwright-report

# 5. Git history: large, and not needed to build or run.
.git

# 6. Files that only matter on the host: docs, CI config, editor and Claude settings.
docs
.github
.claude
